  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1112 PM CDT Mon Jul 27 2026\n\nScattered evening thunderstorms which developed ahead of an \nupper level disturbance moving around an unseasonably warm upper\nlevel high located over northern New Mexico. These storms were \noccurring along and north of a surface boundary extending from \nnorth central Kansas into eastern Colorado. While this \nconvection is expected to weaken overnight, a few isolated to\nscattered thunderstorms will still be possible through Tuesday \nmorning, mainly near the I-70 corridor as the upper level wave \ncrosses western Kansas and as the surface boundary moves across \nsouthwest Kansas. Severe weather risk through Tuesday morning is\nnot expected but some isolated gusty winds will be possible.\n\nDuring the day on Tuesday this surface boundary will remain \nnearly stationary south of a Stafford to Dodge City to Liberal \nline. This will result in keeping the the warmest 850mb \ntemperatures (+30C) confined to areas near the Oklahoma border \nand south central Kansas. As a result highs are once again \nexpected to climb back into the 100 to near 105 degree range in \nthis area with afternoon heat index values between 105 and 110 \nbeing possible, mainly along the Oklahoma border. Latest models \nremain similar to previous runs on the location of this wedge of\nwarmer air so the current heat advisory that was issued earlier\ntoday still looks on track (roughly along and south of a \nStafford to Liberal line). Whether Dodge City, Hugoton or \nElkhart will meet heat advisory criteria remains unclear. \nCurrently looks very marginal so we will continue to monitor \nthis closely overnight and adjust the advisory as needed. \nDespite these areas not being under a Head Advisory on \nTuesday...anyone with outdoor activities should continue to \npractice heat safety. Further north models suggest that 850mb \ntemperatures will be between 3 and 6C cooler so a break in the \n105 to 110 degree heat can be expected during the day. Even so, \nafternoon temperatures will still range from the upper 90s to \naround 100.\n\nScattered thunderstorm will again be possible Tuesday night as \nanother upper level wave moves out of the Central Rockies and \napproaches southwest Kansas along a developing mid level \nbaroclinic zone where a low level moisture axis is forecast to \nbe located. These storms are expected to weaken as they cross \nsouthwest Kansas overnight, however am a little concerned that \nthe models might be weakening/dissipating these storms too \nquickly given the moisture axis over southwest Kansas Tuesday \nnight. At this time confidence is not high enough to deviate far\nfrom the current model forecast and base on the models timing \nof the upper level wave it currently appears that storms will be\nlikely west of highway 83 before midnight. These storms will \nthen weaken as they move further east with the best chance of \novernight storms being north of a Garden City to Larned line. \nLocally heavy rainfall may accompany a few of these storms \nTuesday night given the forecast precipitable water (PWAT) \nvalues of 1.5\" or greater, especially near the Colorado border.\nIsolated gusty winds also can not be completely rule out.\n\nMid week the upper level high over the Central and Southern \nRockies will weaken and shift westward as a more significant \nupper trough rotates around this upper ridge. This setup will \nprovide another opportunity for scattered thunderstorms across \nsouthwest Kansas Wednesday night as the upper level trough \napproaches from the west. Chance for convection appear highest \nwest of highway 83 before midnight, with this activity spreading\nacross the remainder of the area through early Thursday \nmorning.\n\nWith the passage of this upper level trough Wednesday night into\nThursday, a cold front is then expected to move into southwest\nKansas late week. This will bring a better chance for a relief\nin the heat to southwest Kansas by Friday but especially over\nthe upcoming weekend. During this period the temperatures will \nbe primarily in the 90s which should be a welcome relief to the \n100 to 110 degree temperatures observed earlier in the work work\nweek.",
